/-!
# `y² = x³ + 89` through irreducible rank-one sources, with no premise
(generated by `python/class_list_cert.py rank1`)

The classes for `k = 89` (`positive_k.json`):
* `(-3, -3, -9, 1)` is irreducible and nonmonic. Its recorded point `(0, 1)` gives `U = (0 1; -1 3)`, `det U = 1`, with `F ∘ U = −u³ + (30) u v² + (-66) v³` (witness normalization); `RankOneSources.K89.source2` proves that form has only `(−1, 0)`, so `F` has only `(0, 1)`.
* `(-3, -3, -6, 4)` is irreducible and nonmonic. Its recorded point `(-1, -2)` gives `U = (1 7; 2 15)`, `det U = 1`, with `F ∘ U = −u³ + (165) u v² + (816) v³` (witness normalization); `RankOneSources.K89.source3` proves that form has only `(−1, 0)`, so `F` has only `(-1, -2)`.
* `(-1, 0, -12, -10)` is irreducible: `F(u, v) = −N((u + 0v) − vz)` in `ℤ[z]`, `z³ = (-12) z + (-10)`. Its only solution `(−1, 0)` comes from `RankOneSources.K89.source0` (unit generation and the Skolem zero set).
* `(-1, 0, -6, -18)` is irreducible: `F(u, v) = −N((u + 0v) − vz)` in `ℤ[z]`, `z³ = (-6) z + (-18)`. Its only solution `(−1, 0)` comes from `RankOneSources.K89.source1` (unit generation and the Skolem zero set).
* `(0, -3, 0, -89)` is reducible, solved by `ReducibleThue` (factor, Bezout pair, integer square root).

The class-list premise is proved by `ClassListProof.classList_of` (parameters by `norm_num`,
transports by the kernel), and `PositiveKCurve.complete_of_sols` reads off the points.
-/

